            "content": [
              {
                "type": "output_text",
                "text": "The latest notable news about NASA’s Mars **Perseverance rover** is that it has been studying a region on the rim of Jezero Crater called the **“Broom Point member,”** where it found evidence that a **245-foot-thick / 75-meter-thick stack of ancient rock** was likely built up by **repeated asteroid impacts**. NASA says these layered rocks may be **more than 3.9 billion years old**, offering a record of very early Martian history【5:0†source】.\n\nPerseverance also recently reached a major mobility milestone: it has driven the equivalent of a **full marathon — 26.2 miles / 42.195 km — on Mars**. It reached that mark on its **1,890th Martian day, or sol**, about **five years and four months** into the mission, much faster than Opportunity took to cover the same distance【5:1†source】.\n\nNASA’s Mars Reconnaissance Orbiter captured Perseverance from orbit near this milestone, showing the rover as a small speck and revealing its tracks across the Martian surface. At the time, Perseverance was operating west of Jezero Crater in a region nicknamed **“Arbot”**【5:1†source】.\n\nIn short: Perseverance is still active, still driving, and now investigating ancient crater-rim rocks that may preserve clues about Mars’ earliest impact history and environments.",
